{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nFound 2026-11-23 PST while fixing immoral-heated-doctorfish (Gerrit/Jira credentials in operator argv). `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` (lines 95 and 174) tells users to run `copilot mcp add ... --header \"Authorization: Bearer $MCP_CLIENT_PAT_COPILOT\"` and `claude mcp add ... --header \"Authorization: Bearer ${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLAUDE}\"`. The double quotes make the shell expand the PAT, so the rebar MCP client PAT is on the `copilot`/`claude` process command line while it runs, where any local user or process monitor can read it, and it is then written in clear into the client's config file.\n\nThis was kept out of immoral-heated-doctorfish because it is a different credential (the MCP client PAT, not a Gerrit or Jira credential) and the fix depends on each vendor CLI's handling of a literal `${VAR}` header placeholder, which needs checking against the Copilot CLI and Claude Code documentation.\n\n## Vendor behaviour (checked 2026-11-23 PST)\n- Claude Code (official MCP docs, code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp): `${VAR}` and `${VAR:-default}` expand in a server's `url` and `headers` for project `.mcp.json` and for local/user entries in `~/.claude.json`; `claude mcp list`/`get` show the reference by name. `claude mcp add-json <name> '<json>'` is the documented way to add an entry from the command line. `MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLI` is not one of the credential names Claude Code blanks in remote headers.\n- GitHub Copilot CLI (installed 1.0.114): the official \"Adding MCP servers\" page documents `copilot mcp add --header \"HEADER: VALUE\"` and the config files, and says nothing about expansion. The maintainer's closing comment on github/copilot-cli#1232 states `${VAR}` expansion works in MCP config value fields including HTTP headers, and changelog 1.0.108 says to \"use ${NAME} to reference a host environment variable\". The committed repository `.mcp.json`, which Copilot already reads, relies on `${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLI}`. Copilot has no `add-json` subcommand.\n- Shell: double quotes around `${VAR}` expand it before the CLI starts, so the PAT lands in argv and is then saved in clear in the client config.\n\n## Approach\nUse the client's own connect-time expansion and stop passing the header through a shell-expanded argument. In `docs/mcp-client-setup.md`:\n- Copilot CLI: drop the `copilot mcp add ... --header \"...$MCP_CLIENT_PAT_COPILOT\"` command. The documented setup is the committed project `.mcp.json` (already `${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLI}`); for a user-level fallback, copy or merge the `examples/mcp-clients/copilot/mcp-config.json` entry into `~/.copilot/mcp-config.json`. The inline JSON snippet uses `${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLI}`, the canonical name `rebar store doctor` checks for Copilot (`CANONICAL_PAT_ENV`), instead of the stale `$MCP_CLIENT_PAT_COPILOT`.\n- Claude Code: replace the `claude mcp add ... --header \"...${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLAUDE}\"` command with `claude mcp add-json rebar '<json>'`, where the single-quoted JSON carries the literal `${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLI}` placeholder that Claude Code expands at connect time; the JSON snippet uses `${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLI}` too.\n- Add one short warning: never pass the PAT as a double-quoted `--header` value, because the shell expands it into argv and the client saves the expanded value.\n- `examples/mcp-clients/claude/.mcp.json` carries the same double-quoted `claude mcp add --header` one-liner in its `$comment`; switch it to the same single-quoted `add-json` form so the copy-ready example does not teach the leak.\n- `tests/unit/test_review_bot_git_credential_argv.py`: delete `_OPERATOR_EXCLUDED` (and its comment and use), so `_operator_files()` scans `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` with the existing patterns, unchanged; add the doc to `test_the_operator_scan_covers_the_files_named_by_the_bug`.\nNo new config key, environment variable, or flag; guard patterns are not loosened.\n\n## Scope\n- `docs/mcp-client-setup.md`\n- `examples/mcp-clients/claude/.mcp.json` (the `$comment` only; the `mcpServers` entry is unchanged)\n- `tests/unit/test_review_bot_git_credential_argv.py`\n\n## Testing\n- Run against the unfixed doc first: with the exclusion removed and the doc unchanged, `test_no_operator_script_or_doc_puts_a_credential_in_argv` fails on `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` (`header-arg`); after the doc rewrite it passes.\n- `test_the_operator_scan_covers_the_files_named_by_the_bug` asserts the doc is scanned, so the exclusion cannot silently return.\n- `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_client_configs.py` keeps passing (the examples' `mcpServers` entries are unchanged and still valid JSON).\n- `make verify` before push (docs drift, guard, lint).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` no longer shows a command that puts the expanded MCP client PAT in a process argv; the documented setup keeps the PAT out of argv (for example a literal `${VAR}` placeholder the client expands at connect time, verified against each client's documentation).\n- [ ] The operator-credential argv guard added by immoral-heated-doctorfish scans `docs/mcp-client-setup.md` and passes."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795445996510672001,"uuid":"a11afb8f-7533-4c66-bd17-49791214213b"}